WEBVTT

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:11.010
Welcome to The Deep Dive. Today we have our hands on a pretty massive, highly detailed document.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.010
Yeah, it's definitely a thick one.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:18.010
Right. It's the October 2026 multi-sectoral advisory board report on Puerto Rico's Medicaid program.

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:23.010
And look, I know, a government financial report sounds like the kind of thing you read when you're trying to fall asleep.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.010
It really does.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.010
Exactly.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.010
But I promise it's not.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:33.010
Exactly. Our mission today for you listening is to uncover this really dramatic story hidden inside all these pages

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:41.010
because it's about a looming 2027 funding cliff that threatens to strip healthcare from up to a million people.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.010
Which is just a massive number.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:49.010
It is. And oddly enough, we're going to explore how the United States government underfunding this program

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.010
might actually be costing American taxpayers more money overall.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.010
Yeah, the paradox there is wild.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.010
It really is.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:03.010
So for anyone interested in economics or healthcare policy or even just, you know, systemic problem solving, this report is an absolute goldmine.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:10.010
To set a quick baseline for you, we are talking about a system that literally holds the lives of a huge portion of the island in its hands.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.010
Right.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:15.010
Right. Right now, roughly 40% of Puerto Rico's population relies on Medicaid.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.010
Wow, 40%.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:24.010
Yeah. And if you compare that to the U.S. average across the 50 states, which sits around 21%, you have almost double the reliance.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.010
Double the reliance, yeah.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:29.010
So this report is basically the definitive data-driven baseline.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:36.010
They are trying to get federal policymakers to understand the unique structural cracks in how this care is funded.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:37.010
Because they're running out of time, right?

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:42.010
Exactly. They are desperately trying to sound the alarm before the clock runs out next year.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:51.010
Okay, let's unpack this because before we can even look at the crisis itself or this impending cliff, we really have to understand the fundamental difference in how the rules are written.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.010
Right. The rules for Puerto Rico versus the 50 states.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:58.010
Yeah, I mean it feels like they are playing two completely different games here.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.010
They really are.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:05.010
And the core issue here comes down to a mechanism called the Federal Medical Assistance Percentage, or FMAP for short.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.010
FMAP, okay.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.010
Yeah, FMAP.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:13.010
This is the formula the federal government uses to determine how much it chips in for a state's Medicaid program.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:14.010
Okay.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.010
For the 50 states, federal funding for Medicaid is open-ended.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.010
Meaning there's no limit?

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.010
Exactly.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:28.010
If a state needs to cover allowable Medicaid costs, say, you know, there's an unexpected spike in a virus or some new expensive treatment hits the market,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:31.010
the federal government matches the state's spending without a cap.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.010
Okay, that makes sense.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:38.010
And depending on the state's economic situation, that federal match can sometimes go up to 83%.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.010
Wow, 83% is huge.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.010
It is.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.010
But Puerto Rico operates under a completely different framework.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.010
Specifically, they fall under a section 1108 capped allotment.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.010
Okay, I want to make sure we visualize this properly for everyone listening.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.010
It's like the states have an open tab at a restaurant, right?

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.010
Yeah, that's a good way to think about it.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:03:02.010
Like if Florida or Texas orders $100 of health care, the federal government covers a strict percentage of that bill no matter what.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.010
Whether the final tab is $10,000 or $10 billion.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.010
Right, the percentage stays the same.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.010
But Puerto Rico isn't given an open tab.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.010
They are handed a fixed-value gift card.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.010
That is the perfect analogy.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:19.010
Because once Puerto Rico hits the limit on that gift card, the federal dollars just stop.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.010
Just completely stop.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.010
Completely.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:26.010
The territory then has to use its own local general funds to cover every single penny of care after that.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.010
Which has to be incredibly difficult.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.010
Oh, it is.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:34.010
Furthermore, their historical base F map, that underlying matching rate before they even hit the cap,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.010
is statutorily locked at a very low 55%.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:46.010
So wait, not only is the total amount capped, but the federal government is only paying 55 cents on the dollar leading up to that cap.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.010
That's right.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.010
I mean, that seems like a structure built to fail considering the economic challenges on the island.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.010
Well, there was a temporary bandage applied recently.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:04:01.010
The Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2023, which people often just call a CAA 2023,

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.010
temporarily bumped Puerto Rico's matching rate up to 76%.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.010
Okay, that's a bit better.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:07.010
It was a big help.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:12.010
It also gave them a larger allotment ceiling, a bigger gift card using your analogy,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:17.010
which gradually steps up to $3.825 billion by fiscal year 2027.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.010
Okay, so a temporary boost.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.010
Right.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.010
And this temporary injection was huge.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:28.010
It allowed Puerto Rico to finally raise their eligibility threshold to 100% of the federal poverty level.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.010
Wait, what was it before?

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:36.010
Prior to this, because funds were so tight, Puerto Rico had to use a local artificially low poverty level.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.010
Oh, wow.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:41.010
Yeah, which meant residents had to be significantly poorer than their counterparts on the mainland,

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.010
just to qualify for the exact same basic medical care.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:48.010
Which fundamentally changes who actually gets to see a doctor.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:49.010
Exactly.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:53.010
But I mean, even with that temporary fix, a cap is still a cap, right?

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:58.010
Like if inflation drives up the cost of medicine or if more people get sick, the cap doesn't care.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:04:59.010
It doesn't care at all.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:04.010
And what's fascinating here is that even with this temporary CAA 2023 boost,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:13.010
Puerto Rico estimates its expenditures for 2026 and 2027 will still exceed that new higher federal capped allotment.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.010
So they're still going to run out of money?

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.010
Yes.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:22.010
The raw cost of providing basic modern medical care is simply outpacing the arbitrary statutory ceiling.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:26.010
And a rigid capped funding structure is dangerous on its own,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:32.010
but it becomes catastrophic when it collides with the reality of the people actually living in the system.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.010
Oh, absolutely.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:37.010
Because the demographic reality in Puerto Rico is shifting drastically,

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:40.010
and that changes the entire mathematical equation for this funding.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:41.010
Yeah.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:44.010
We are basically looking at a demographic perfect storm.

