The outmigration from Puerto Rico to the States is profound.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.010
And health is a primary driver.

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.010
The report mentioned some survey data on this, right?

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:44.010
Yes.

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:47.010
A 2023 survey by Ipsos of 400 adults

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.010
who had recently relocated to the mainland

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:52.010
revealed that 19% cited health services

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.010
as a top five reason for moving.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:57.010
And 14% moved specifically to, quote,

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:58.010
treat a health condition.

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:01.010
They aren't just moving for better jobs or lower taxes.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:04.010
They are packing up their lives and moving across an ocean

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.010
just to get the care they need to stay alive.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.010
And here is the financial irony for the federal government.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:10.010
Okay, let's hear it.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:12.010
Even though things like groceries, utilities,

00:14:12.010 --> 00:14:15.010
and consumer goods are notoriously expensive in Puerto Rico

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:17.010
due to import laws,

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:20.010
health care services themselves are actually much cheaper

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.010
on the island than on the mainland.

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:24.010
Really? How much cheaper?

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:27.010
Well, in 2024, the total Medicaid benefit spending

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.010
per enrollee in Puerto Rico

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.010
was about $3,293 for the year.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:34.010
Okay, so about $3,300.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:36.010
What does that same patient cost

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:38.010
if they move to the mainland?

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:43.010
The median state on the mainland costs $10,377 per enrollee.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:44.010
Wow.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.010
It is over three times as expensive.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:50.010
So when an estimated 15,303 Medicaid-eligible individuals

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.010
relocated from Puerto Rico to states like Florida,

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:56.010
Texas, and Pennsylvania in 2024,

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:59.010
they automatically qualified for state Medicaid.

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:00.010
Because they are U.S. citizens.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:01.010
Exactly.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:04.010
Here's where it gets really interesting.

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:06.010
If the federal government is paying three times as much

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:09.010
for a patient in Florida compared to Puerto Rico,

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:11.010
is it actually mathematically cheaper

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:14.010
for the federal government to just give Puerto Rico

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:15.010
the funding it's asking for

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.010
rather than underfunding them

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:20.010
and forcing this medical migration?

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.010
The report actually crunched the numbers on this,

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:24.010
and the math is undeniable.

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:27.010
Covering those 15,000-plus relocated individuals

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:29.010
on the mainland cost the federal government

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:32.010
an estimated $96.8 million in a single year.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:33.010
Okay.

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:35.010
If those exact same individuals had been able

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.010
to stay in Puerto Rico and receive care there,

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:41.010
the federal cost would have been $41.8 million.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:42.010
That is wild.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.010
I mean, the federal government is paying

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:46.010
a massive arbitrage premium

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:49.010
just because a citizen changed their zip code.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:50.010
Exactly.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:53.010
By actively underfunding the island's health care system,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.010
the federal government actually spent

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:57.010
an additional $55 million in a single year.

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.010
Just wasted money.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.010
They paid a 2.3 times higher premium

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:03.010
for the exact same citizens.

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.010
It's a staggering inefficiency.

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:08.010
And remember, that is just a one-year snapshot

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.010
of new migrants.

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.010
It doesn't account for the compounding cumulative costs

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:14.010
of the tens of thousands of people

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:16.010
who migrated for health reasons in all the prior years.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:18.010
So knowing that underfunding Puerto Rico

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:21.010
causes this incredibly costly ripple effect

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:22.010
across the 50 states,

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:24.010
we must look at what happens

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:27.010
when the current temporary funding expires next year.

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:28.010
This is the really scary part.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:30.010
The report calls it the 2027 cliff,

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.010
and it just sounds apocalyptic

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.010
for the health care infrastructure.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:36.010
The impending disaster is very real.

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:38.010
In fiscal year 2028,

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.010
Puerto Rico's FMAP, that federal matching rate

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:43.010
we talked about, will legally revert to 55%.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.010
Ouch.

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:47.010
Furthermore, the allotment ceiling will plummet

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:50.010
back down to a baseline tied to 2019 levels.

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:53.010
In practical terms, this equates to an annual drop

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:57.010
in federal funding of approximately $3.36 billion.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:00.010
Over $3 billion evaporating

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.010
from the health care system overnight.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.010
Yes.

00:17:03.010 --> 00:17:05.010
I mean, you can't just trim the fat

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:06.010
to survive a cut like that.

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:08.010
You are amputating limbs at that point.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:11.010
The human toll of that drop is dire.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:14.010
The projections show that anywhere from 860,000

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.010
to 1 million beneficiaries

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:18.010
could lose their health care coverage entirely.

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:20.010
A million people.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:22.010
That's essentially a quarter of the island's population

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:24.010
suddenly without access to a doctor.

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:25.010
Right.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.010
The island's uninsured rate would skyrocket

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:31.010
from its current 5.7% to roughly 30%.

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:32.010
That's devastating.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:34.010
Furthermore, those recent additions to coverage

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.010
that were just added in 2024,

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:38.010
critical things like home health,

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:40.010
durable medical equipment, hospice care,

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:43.010
and non-emergency transportation

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:45.010
would all be in immediate jeopardy.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:47.010
Which are the exact specific services

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:50.010
a rapidly aging, chronically ill population

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.010
desperately needs to stay out of the ER.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:53.010
Exactly.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.010
Okay, I have to ask a skeptical question here

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:57.010
because I know it's on your mind

00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:58.010
as you listen to this.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:17:59.010
Go for it.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:01.010
Whenever we talk about massive funding gaps

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:02.010
in government programs,

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.010
critics usually point to, you know,

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:06.010
local mismanagement.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.010
People are going to wonder,

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:10.010
is Puerto Rico actually managing these funds properly

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:13.010
or is this just a leaky bucket situation?

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.010
That's a very common question.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:16.010
Because if the system is fundamentally broken

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:17.010
and inefficient,

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.010
just throwing billions of dollars at it

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:20.010
won't actually fix the care.

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:22.010
It is a very fair critique,

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:23.010
often leveled to territories.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.010
And the report actually dedicates

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:27.010
an extensive, highly technical section

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:29.010
to system modernization and compliance

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.010
precisely to clear this up.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:32.010
Okay, what do they say?

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.010
Puerto Rico has actually met all

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:36.010
Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:39.010
or CMS integrity requirements.

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:41.010
They aren't just holding out their hands for cash.

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:42.010
They are actively building

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:44.010
the modern digital infrastructure

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.010
required to manage these funds efficiently.

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:48.010
So what kind of infrastructure are we talking about?

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:50.010
Like how are they proving they aren't a leaky bucket?

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:52.010
Well, they've completely overhauled

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.010
how they track data and money.

00:18:54.010 --> 00:18:55.010
For one, they successfully launched

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:57.010
an enterprise data warehouse

00:18:57.010 --> 00:18:59.010
in February 2026.

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:00.010
An enterprise data warehouse.

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:01.010
Yeah.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:02.010
Okay.

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:04.010
Think of this as a massive,

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:06.010
centralized digital nervous system

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:08.010
that links all patient demographics

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.010
and billing together.

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:11.010
It makes it incredibly difficult

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:14.010
to double bill or lose track of patient outcomes.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:15.010
Okay, that's a good step.

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:18.010
They also implemented an asset verification system.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:21.010
This digitally pings financial institutions

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.010
in real time to check bank accounts

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:25.010
ensuring people aren't hiding wealth

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:26.010
to get benefits,

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:28.010
which instantly cuts down on fraud.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.010
So they are actively modernizing

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:33.010
the guardrails to protect the federal dollars.

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:34.010
Exactly.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:35.010
Okay.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:36.010
And on the hospital side,

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:38.010
they are utilizing a new diagnosis-related

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.010
group-based payment system, or DRG.

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:42.010
How does that work?

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:44.010
Instead of a hospital billing Medicaid

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:46.010
for every individual bandage,

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:48.010
aspirin, and hour of a nurse's time,

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:50.010
they get a flat fixed rate

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:52.010
based on the patient's diagnosis.

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:53.010
Oh, I see.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:55.010
So if a patient comes in for a heart attack,

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:57.010
the hospital gets a set amount

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:59.010
to treat that heart attack.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:00.010
This forces the hospital

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:02.010
to be incredibly efficient with their resources,

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:05.010
rather than just running up and open in a tab.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:07.010
That makes total sense.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:08.010
And because of these rigorous,

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:10.010
proven program integrity measures,

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.010
they've actually been awarded

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:14.010
an extra $375 million annually

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:16.010
by the federal government purely

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:17.010
for their high compliance.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:18.010
Wait, really?

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:19.010
So they are actively being rewarded

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:20.010
for good management

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:22.010
by the exact same federal government

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:23.010
that caps their funding.

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:24.010
Precisely.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:25.010
Mm-hmm.

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:26.010
It's a bit ironic.

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.010
And they are using the funds they do have

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.010
to take aggressive steps

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:31.010
to stabilize their provider network.

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:32.010
How so?

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:34.010
They are now reimbursing local doctors

00:20:34.010 --> 00:20:38.010
at 100% of the Medicare Part B fee schedule.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:41.010
This is a direct, calculated move

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:43.010
to stop the brain drain of physicians

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:44.010
who are leaving the island

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:46.010
because they simply couldn't make a living.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:47.010
Wow.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:48.010
The data clearly shows

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:50.010
it is not a management issue.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.010
It is a mathematical impossibility

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:55.010
to sustain care under the statutory cap.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:57.010
The bucket isn't leaky.

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:59.010
The bucket just isn't big enough

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.010
to hold the water they need.

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:03.010
So what does this all mean?

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:05.010
Wrapping up this deep dive for you,

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:07.010
it becomes incredibly clear

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:09.010
that the Puerto Rico Medicaid report

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:11.010
isn't just a localized plea for cash.

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:12.010
Not at all.

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:14.010
It is a highly detailed,

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:17.010
structural analysis of an uneven playing field.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.010
We have this perfect storm,

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:20.010
a capped funding model

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.010
combined with rapid aging,

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:23.010
incredibly high poverty,

00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:25.010
and unequal disability benefits.

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:28.010
And the storm is driving a medical exodus

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:29.010
of patients to the mainland,

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:31.010
which in a twisted bit of irony,

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:33.010
ultimately costs American taxpayers

00:21:33.010 --> 00:21:35.010
significantly more money overall.

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:37.010
It's just a cycle of inefficiency.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.010
So the multi-sectoral boards asked to Congress

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:41.010
is straightforward, if ambitious.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:43.010
They want them to remove

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:46.010
the section 1108 allotment ceiling entirely.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:49.010
They want to apply the standard state F-Map formula,

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:51.010
so Puerto Rico gets the 83% match

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:53.010
they mathematically qualify for.

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.010
And they want to include them

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:57.010
in disproportionate share hospital payments

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:59.010
to stabilize their bleeding trauma centers.

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:01.010
And if we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:22:01.010 --> 00:22:03.010
I want to leave you with a final thought

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:04.010
to mull over today.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.010
Good.

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:07.010
We often frame federal budget caps

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:09.010
in political debates as a way to save money

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:11.010
or enforce fiscal discipline.

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:13.010
It sounds incredibly responsible on paper.

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:15.010
Sure, it plays well on the news.

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:17.010
But if setting an arbitrary ceiling

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:19.010
on Puerto Rico's health care funding

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:21.010
simply forces those exact same citizens

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.010
to move to states where their health care costs

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:25.010
the federal government two to three times as much,

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:27.010
we have to ask ourselves a difficult question.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:28.010
What's that?

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:30.010
Are territorial budget caps

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:32.010
actually saving the government money?

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:34.010
Or are they just a bureaucratic trick

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:37.010
to hide the true cost of care on someone else's balance sheet?

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:39.010
It is a phenomenal question.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:41.010
Because when you look closely

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:44.010
at the mechanisms of health care policy,

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.010
trying to cap the cost of keeping humans alive

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:48.010
doesn't erase the expense.

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:50.010
It just shifts the burden.

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:52.010
And usually at a premium.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:54.010
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:57.010
Keep questioning the structures behind the headlines.

